<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Famous Panamanians</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Favorite Athletes!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Show comprehension of the main plot and characters in short stories. Reading: Summarize sections of texts in written form. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by reading base words and inflectional endings (-s, -es, -ed, -ing).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can show comprehension of the plot in a short story (e.g., "This is Adalberto Carrasquilla. He is one of the best soccer players in Panama.")
            Can summarize and extract key details from a text about the achievements of famous people (e.g., "She is Atheyna Bylon. She is a famous boxer and an Olympic medalist.")
            Can read sentences fluently by recognizing and pronouncing base words with inflectional endings (-ed, -ing), demonstrating phonemic awareness (e.g., "He improved his own marks.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of famous Panamanian athletes (e.g., Roberto Durán, Mariano Rivera, Atheyna Bylon, Adalberto Carrasquilla). Students read the names and sports silently. The teacher asks, "Who are these people? What are they famous for?" encouraging students to use vocabulary from Lesson 1 (e.g., <i>athlete, famous, win, award</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models reading simple sentences about one athlete, pointing to the words, for example: "This is Roberto Durán. He is a famous boxer. He <b>won</b> many <b>awards</b>. Many sports fans are <b>watching</b> his old fights." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of "-ed" in 'won' (although it's irregular, the concept of past tense achievement) and "-ing" in 'watching'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is Roberto Durán an actor or an athlete?" (Athlete)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'famous' mean? Does everyone know him?" (Yes, very well-known)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Famous Panamanians".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short biographical texts/profiles (narrative excerpts) about two Panamanian athletes: Atheyna Bylon and Adalberto Carrasquilla. The texts will be displayed on the board or in a handout, featuring the target grammar (Present continuous for future events, Comparatives, Past simple for achievements) and vocabulary (<i>athlete, achievement, award, competition, perform, win, inspire, train, famous, talented, skilled, more, best</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example text snippets:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"<b>Atheyna Bylon: The Talented Boxer</b><br> Atheyna Bylon is a very <b>famous</b> boxer and a <b>talented athlete</b> from Panama. She is an Olympic medalist. Atheyna <b>won</b> a medal in the Olympic Games, which was a great <b>achievement</b> for our country. She is highly <b>skilled</b> in her sport. Currently, she is <b>training</b> for a new tournament. Her performance always inspires young people. Many sports fans consider her one of the <b>best</b> female boxers."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"<b>Adalberto Carrasquilla: The Skilled Soccer Player</b><br> Adalberto Carrasquilla is a <b>skilled</b> soccer player and an important member of Panama's national team. He is <b>more popular</b> than many other young players. Adalberto always <b>improved</b> his own marks during <b>competition</b>. He plays with great energy and <b>inspires</b> his teammates. He is <b>considered</b> one of the <b>best</b> soccer players in Panama today. People love to watch him <b>perform</b> on the field."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing achievements of celebrities and developing reports on notable figures (e.g., "She is Atheyna Bylon. She is a famous boxer and an Olympic medalist.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the texts quickly to answer: "Who are Atheyna and Adalberto? What sports do they play?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Students read statements about the texts and determine if they are true or false (e.g., "Atheyna Bylon won a medal." "Adalberto Carrasquilla is a singer.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> Students read and match each athlete to their main achievement mentioned in the text (e.g., Atheyna Bylon – Olympic Medalist; Adalberto Carrasquilla – Important National Team Member).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension, identifying key details, understanding grammar structures (Past simple for achievements, Comparatives and superlatives, Present continuous for future events) and target vocabulary (<i>athlete, achievement, award, competition, perform, win, inspire, train, famous, popular, talented, skilled, more, best</i>). Phonemic awareness: reading inflectional endings (-ed, -ing).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Cloze Reading:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences from the texts about Atheyna and Adalberto, but with key vocabulary words missing. Students read the sentences and fill in the blanks using words from a word bank (e.g., <i>won, training, famous, achievement, best, improved</i>). Example: "Atheyna Bylon ______ a medal." "She is ______ for a new tournament." "He always ______ his own marks."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Sentence Transformation & Reading:</b> Students read given sentences and transform them using comparatives or superlatives based on information from the text or general knowledge (from Lesson 1). Then, they read the new sentences aloud, focusing on fluency and pronunciation of words like "improved," "trained," "watching."
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "Adalberto Carrasquilla is popular. Roberto Durán is _______ (popular) Adalberto." (<i>more popular than</i>)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "Roberto Durán is considered one of the _______ boxers in the world." (<i>greatest/best</i>)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Reading & Peer Check:</b> In pairs, students take turns reading the original texts or the completed sentences from the previous activities. Their partner listens for correct pronunciation of the inflectional endings (-ed, -ing) and helps correct any errors in reading fluently. For instance, paying attention to "won," "improved," "training," "watching," "performed."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Famous Panamanians."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format: Summarizing Athlete Profiles</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work individually to summarize the main achievements and characteristics of one of the athletes they read about (Atheyna Bylon or Adalberto Carrasquilla) using a graphic organizer or a short summary sheet. They will extract key details from the texts to complete this task. After completing their summaries, students will form new pairs. Each student will *read* their summary aloud to their partner, and the partner will listen and ask one comprehension question (e.g., "What sport does Adalberto play?" "What did Atheyna win?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> This activity directly connects to "My Favorite Athletes!" and "Famous Panamanians" by requiring students to identify and articulate key information about these figures, specifically focusing on their achievements.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in reading extracted information, showing comprehension of the main plot and characters, and summarizing key details. Collaborative interaction through reading and questioning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation during the pair reading and question-answer activity. The teacher will listen for comprehension and pronunciation. A short individual reading comprehension quiz will also be administered.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will individually read a short, new paragraph (2-3 sentences) about another Panamanian athlete (e.g., Irving Saladino, or a more general text about a typical athlete's career). After reading, they will answer two comprehension questions in written form or verbally, demonstrating their ability to summarize main ideas and extract key details. They will also complete 2-3 True/False statements related to the text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension:</b> Ability to accurately answer comprehension questions and identify true/false statements from the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Summarization:</b> Ability to extract and summarize key details (e.g., name, sport, main achievement).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation (Phonemic Awareness):</b> Intelligible pronunciation of base words with inflectional endings (-ed, -ing) when reading answers or summaries aloud (if verbal assessment).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Integration of relevant vocabulary from the lesson (e.g., <i>athlete, achievement, win, famous</i>).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One interesting fact I read today was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can now read and understand texts about famous people's ______ (achievements/sports)."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading about famous athletes did I find easiest or most difficult today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which new words did I understand better after reading the texts?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., Student engagement with the topic, clarity of the texts, effectiveness of comprehension tasks.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with reading comprehension or phonemic awareness (e.g., more guided reading, explicit pronunciation drills for -ed/-ing)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I prepare students for the next lesson (Speaking & Interaction)? (e.g., Ensure students have internalized key facts about the athletes, and are comfortable using the target vocabulary and grammar to express opinions and compare figures).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will actively use the information they read today to discuss, compare, and present information about these famous Panamanians, focusing on expressing their opinions and describing achievements verbally.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>